\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 <!$Scr_H::2>
\f0 You have an unusual advantage and an unusual danger in exactly the same place.\
The advantage is that you seem to have enough genuine subject matter, ongoing work, and personal motivation to supply years of programming without having to invent a fake entertainment persona.\
The danger is that the undertaking may be so encompassing, and its internal meanings so developed, that an audience has difficulty discovering what makes any particular afternoon worth watching.\
My preferred solution is not a rigid format. It's a very lightweight production discipline:\
Every broadcast is allowed to be whatever your life and work require that day. But every broadcast should help a newcomer understand where they are, what's happening, and what makes it interesting.\
And, separately, every week or two, something should emerge that a complete stranger can appreciate without having watched the rest.\
That could be an invention, a joke, a surprisingly beautiful conversation, a provocative question, a demonstration, or a short film assembled from the livestream.\
If the show becomes successful, I suspect it won't be because you persuaded the world that your grandest theory is already correct.\
It will be because people found spending time in the Wilder-ness interesting enough to return, and some of them discovered they could make meaningful contributions to what was being built there.\
The strongest version of this is not a livestream about inventing peace. It's an ongoing, publicly observable attempt to invent things\'97with peace as the motivating objective.\
That is the production I'd be interested in watching develop.\
